Typical six-sided "Monopoly" dice are not fair, either.  In fact, a
friend of mine once did a demonstration in a high school math class
(after a weekend of kicking tail at Risk) where he would toss two
separate dies, one would be rolled, the other he would lob in the air.
 He did each one 1,000 times.  I don't remember the exact numbers, but
the rolling was pretty close to an even distribution.  The lobbed dice
resulted in an a result of "6" almost 25% of the time, with the
numbers tapering down to "1".  I remember 3 was about even with 4,
maybe even greater.
The reason, he said was that the divots drilled for the dots was
enough of a weighting factor to cause the 6, being lighter, to be on
top.  I've not actually tried doing this in Risk, but I imagine you
could probably march your way across Europe faster than Hitler's
Panzers.

